{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["dom", "dom.iterable", "esnext"],
    "allowJs": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "jsx": "preserve",
    "incremental": true,
    "plugins": [
      {
        "name": "next"
      }
    ],
    "paths": {
{{- if .Workspaces}}
      "@/components/ui/*": ["../../packages/ui-web/src/components/ui/*"],
{{- end}}
{{- range .WebRuntimeTypePins}}
      "{{.}}": ["./node_modules/{{.}}"],
{{- end}}
      "@/*": ["./src/*"]
    }
  },
  "// peer dedupe note": "The @connectrpc/@bufbuild/@opentelemetry/@tanstack entries in paths pin @reliantlabs/forge-web-runtime's peerDependencies to THIS app's copy, for the same reason next.config.ts redirects them in the webpack resolver. @tanstack/react-query is listed because the generated hooks EXPOSE its types (UseQueryOptions, ThrowOnError, Query) in their own signatures, so two copies fail with 'Property #private in type Query refers to a different member'; query-core is listed with it because react-query re-exports those types FROM it, and pinning only the wrapper leaves the underlying copy split. A dev build of forge bridges the runtime with a `file:` specifier, which npm materialises as a symlink into the forge checkout; that checkout carries its own node_modules, so the runtime's bare imports resolve there while this app's resolve here. The bundler fix in next.config.ts does not help `tsc --noEmit`, which does not run webpack: typecheck saw two nominally-distinct copies of the same package and failed with TS2322 'Type Transport is not assignable to type Transport' in src/lib/mock-transport_gen.ts. The list is DERIVED from the runtime's declared peerDependencies (internal/webruntimepeers) and rendered from WebRuntimeTypePins, so there is no list here to keep in step. This note sits at the top level, NOT inside compilerOptions, because tsc validates that object and rejects an unknown key there with TS5023.",
  "include": ["next-env.d.ts", "**/*.ts", "**/*.tsx"],
  "// exclude note": "Exclude the Next build dirs wholesale (not just subpaths). Next writes per-route type stubs under .next{,-prod}/types that import the route module by path; deleting a scaffolded route leaves a STALE stub whose import now dangles, so `tsc --noEmit` would spew TS2307 for a route that no longer exists. .next-prod is forge's production distDir (see next.config.ts). Excluding both wins even when `next dev`/`next build` re-adds .next/types/**/*.ts to `include` (TypeScript's exclude beats include).",
  "exclude": ["node_modules", ".next", ".next-prod"]
}
